WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Europe's diesel tax breaks cut CO2 but filled city streets with nitrogen dioxide instead.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.800
This is unintended consequences, episode 130.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:17.060
In the late 1990s, European policy makers faced rising concern over greenhouse gas emissions

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.220
from road transport.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.080
They viewed diesel engines as a ready technical solution because those engines delivered more

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.760
kilometers per liter of fuel, and therefore emitted less CO2 per kilometer than comparable

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:36.420
petrol engines. The arithmetic was straightforward. A vehicle that travels the same distance on less

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:43.920
fuel releases fewer carbon atoms into the atmosphere. The United Kingdom's 2002 company car tax reform

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:50.440
formalized this logic by tying the tax rate directly to official CO2 grams per kilometer figures.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:56.560
At the time, regulators treated the lower CO2 number as the decisive environmental metric

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.320
and did not assign equal weight to other tailpipe pollutants.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.780
Most Western European countries reduced excise duty on diesel relative to petrol and adjusted

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.740
annual circulation taxes to favor diesel models.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.960
In the United Kingdom, the 2002 company car tax change accelerated the shift among business

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.520
fleets, which in turn influenced the wider used car market.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.260
Early data showed the intended result.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:28.240
Diesel's share of new car registrations in Western Europe climbed from under 25% to

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.580
more than 50% by 2011.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.960
Proponents pointed to measurable reductions in fleet average CO2 and welcomed the contribution

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.620
to national climate targets.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.420
A few air quality specialists noted that diesel combustion produced substantially more nitrogen

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.660
oxygen oxides, and particulate matter, yet these warnings received limited policy attention

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.420
while the CO2 metric remained the dominant target.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.420
Diesel engines emit several times more nitrogen oxides under real-world driving conditions

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.680
than the laboratory test cycles used for type approval.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.600
Because the tax incentives rapidly increased the number of diesel vehicles on the road, total

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.780
NOX output from passenger cars rose even as CO2 declined.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:21.220
When diesel's share moved from roughly 25% to over 50%, the proportion of higher NOX vehicles in the fleet approximately doubled.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:29.040
Each additional diesel car replaced a lower NOX petrol car, so the aggregate emissions of nitrogen oxides increased,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.060
even though individual vehicles met the CO2-based tax criteria.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.580
Roadside monitoring stations in London, Paris, Berlin, and other major cities began recording persistent breaches

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.820
of the European Union nitrogen dioxide limit values.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.200
The 2015 Volkswagen emissions scandal later revealed that many diesel cars had been equipped

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:56.100
with software that reduced NOX only during official tests, confirming that real-world emissions

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.000
were often several times higher than certified figures.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:05.300
The resulting pollution contributed to respiratory health burdens in densely populated urban corridors.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.960
wars. Cities responded with low emission zones and later diesel scrapage schemes, yet

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.520
the fleet composition created by more than a decade of favorable taxation could not

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.220
be altered overnight. Second order effects included accelerated turnover of older diesel

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:26.320
vehicles into secondary markets in Eastern Europe and pressure on public budgets to fund

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.120
new mitigation infrastructure. Third order effects appeared in municipal health spending and

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.780
and in the uneven distribution of air quality improvements across neighborhoods.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:41.640
By the mid-2000s, tens several of the same governments that had introduced the tax advantages

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:47.660
began reversing course through higher diesel taxes, city center bands, and purchase incentives

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.560
for petrol and electric alternatives.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.520
Scrapage programs offered payments to owners willing to retire older diesel cars, while low

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.620
emission zones restricted access for higher polluting models.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:04.420
vehicles. These corrective measures themselves generated new distributional effects, disproportionately

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:09.040
affecting lower income households that had purchased the once subsidized diesel vehicles.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:14.980
Air quality data in some cities improved gradually after the fleet began to turn over, yet

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:20.440
legacy effects on public health and municipal spending persisted. Incentive structures that

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.780
optimize for one measurable outcome tend to shift unmeasured burdens elsewhere in the system.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:32.740
When regulators anchor policy on a narrow proxy such as CO2 grams per kilometer, they create

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:38.120
strong economic signals that later prove difficult to unwind once capital stock has adjusted.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:44.540
Complex technical systems therefore reward decision-makers who examine the full set of pollutants

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:49.100
and real-world operating conditions rather than laboratory test values alone.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.180
The same pattern appears today whenever subsidies or taxes are tied to a single performance

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.880
number without parallel safeguards for other externalities.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.800
What safeguards would you want to see if a new subsidy program were proposed that rewarded

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.100
only one narrow environmental metric?

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.600
And that's where today's story leaves us.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.920
If it changed how you see one everyday decision, that's the whole point.

